import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var a={electricField:{theory:{concept:"[[An electric field is the field through which electric charges act on one another: every charge creates a field around itself, and the field exerts a force on any other charge placed in it.]] At each point the field is described by its strength E = F / q \u2014 the force on a small positive test charge divided by that charge. E is a vector, measured in N/C (= V/m)."},section:{idea:{title:"The idea of a field",text:"Instead of asking how two charges act on each other, we ask what the first charge does to the space around it. [[Once you know the field, you know the force on any charge: F = q\xB7E \u2014 along the field for a positive charge, against it for a negative one.]]"},point:{title:"The field of a point charge",text:"[[A point charge Q makes a field of size E = k\xB7Q/r\xB2, where r is the distance from it.]] The field points straight away from a positive charge and straight towards a negative one. It gets weaker quickly: twice as far, a quarter of the field. Drag the test charge outward and watch the E arrow shrink and the force with it."},lines:{title:"Field lines",text:"Field lines are drawn so that the field is always along the line, and the dots moving along them show the direction a positive charge would go: lines start on positive charges and end on negative ones (or run off to infinity). They never cross, and where they crowd together the field is strong. The lines are only a way to draw the field: the field also exists between them."},superposition:{title:"Several charges",text:"[[When there are several charges, the field at a point is the vector sum of the fields of all of them: E = E\u2081 + E\u2082 + \u2026.]] Between two equal like charges the fields point in opposite directions and cancel exactly in the middle: there is a point where the field is zero. Between opposite charges the fields point the same way and add up, so the field is strongest there."},dipole:{title:"Dipole and quadrupole",text:"Two equal charges of opposite sign form a dipole: the lines go from the + charge to the \u2212 charge, and far away the field falls as 1/r\xB3 (faster than a single charge, since the two almost cancel). A water molecule is a tiny dipole. Four charges + \u2212 \u2212 + in a square form a quadrupole, whose field falls even faster."},plates:{title:"Two charged plates",text:"Between two large parallel plates with opposite charges the field is uniform: the same size and direction everywhere, E \u2248 \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080, where \u03C3 is the charge per area. Outside the plates it is almost zero, except near the edges where the lines bulge. Such a capacitor is the easiest way to make a known, uniform field; a charge in it is pushed with a constant force."},motion:{title:"A charge in the field",text:"Release a charge and it accelerates: a = qE/m. A positive charge follows the direction of the field, a negative charge goes the other way, and a heavier one accelerates less. In a uniform field the acceleration is constant (like a falling stone); near a point charge it grows as the charge comes closer. Because the real motion is extremely fast, the simulation slows the time down."},energy:{title:"Potential and energy",text:"A positive charge Q has a potential V = kQ/r at the distance r: the energy per unit charge. A test charge has the potential energy U = q\xB7V, and while it moves the sum K + U = \xBDmv\xB2 + qV stays the same \u2014 the field changes one kind of energy into the other (the graph shows it). The field is the steepness of the potential: it points downhill, from high V to low V."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"The distance from a point charge doubles. What happens to the field? (It becomes 4 times weaker.) In which direction does the force act on a negative test charge placed in the field of a positive one? (Towards the positive charge.) Where is the field zero between two equal positive charges? (Exactly in the middle.) What is special about the field between two parallel plates? (It is uniform.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{definition:"The field is the force per unit of charge",point:"The field of a point charge Q at the distance r",force:"The force on a charge q in the field E",acceleration:"The acceleration of a charge of mass m",superposition:"The field of several charges is the vector sum",plates:"The field between two large plates with surface charge \u03C3 is uniform",potential:"The potential of a point charge, and the energy of a test charge",energy:"Conservation of energy of a charge in the field"},variable:{E:"Electric field strength",F:"Force on the test charge",q:"Charge of the test charge",r:"Distance from the charge",V:"Electric potential",m:"Mass of the test charge"},assumption:{fixed:"The source charges are fixed points and do not move.",light:"The test charge is so small that it does not disturb the field; only the electric force acts on it (no gravity, no air).",plates:"The plates are shown in cross-section: each is a row of long charged rods, whose field gives the uniform field between the plates.",scale:"Not to scale: the dots show direction only, and arrow lengths follow the size of the field on a logarithmic scale (the numbers give the exact values). The motion is slowed down, since real accelerations are enormous."},param:{config:"Source charges",charge:"Size of the charges",mode:"Test charge",probeX:"Test charge: position x",probeY:"Test charge: position y",testCharge:"Charge of the test charge",separation:"Distance between the charges (gap between the plates)",mass:"Mass of the test charge",timeScale:"Slow motion (real seconds per second)",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{config:"A single charge, a pair, four charges or two charged plates.",charge:"The size of each source charge; for the plates, of each plate.",mode:"Held in place: only the field and the force are measured. Released: it starts from rest and moves.",probe:"In the picture the origin is the middle. In release mode this is where the charge starts.",testCharge:"A positive test charge goes with the field, a negative one against it.",separation:"Used for a pair, four charges and the plates.",mass:"A heavier charge accelerates less.",timeScale:"Small values make the motion slow enough to follow."},config:{positive:"One positive charge",negative:"One negative charge",dipole:"Dipole: + and \u2212",like:"Two positive charges",quadrupole:"Four charges + \u2212 \u2212 +",plates:"Two charged plates"},preset:{small:"Small",medium:"Medium",large:"Large",probe:"Held in place",release:"Released from rest",left:"Left",middle:"Middle",right:"Right",bottom:"Bottom",top:"Top",negative:"\u22121 \xB5C",neutral:"Neutral",positive:"+1 \xB5C"},measure:{field:"Field strength at the test charge",angle:"Direction of the field (from the x axis)",force:"Force on the test charge",fieldX:"Field component along x",fieldY:"Field component along y",acceleration:"Acceleration of the test charge",nearest:"Distance to the nearest charge",simTime:"Time since release",potential:"Potential at the test charge",potentialEnergy:"Potential energy of the test charge",kinetic:"Kinetic energy",total:"Total energy K + U",uniformity:"Field compared with the middle of the gap"},view:{positive:"The field of a positive charge points straight away from it",negative:"The field of a negative charge points straight towards it",dipole:"Dipole: the lines run from the + charge to the \u2212 charge",like:"Two like charges: the lines push apart; the field is zero in the middle",quadrupole:"Four charges: the lines run from + to the nearest \u2212",plates:"Two charged plates: the field between them is uniform (top plate +)",legend:"Dots flowing along the lines: the way a positive charge would move",linesOption:"Field lines",arrowsOption:"Field and force at the test charge",vectorsOption:"Arrows over the whole picture"},graph:{field:"Field at the test charge against time",speed:"Speed against time",energy:"Energy of the test charge (the sum stays constant)"},challenge:{repelled:{title:"Pushed away",description:"Around one positive charge, release a positive test charge and let it be pushed out of the picture.",hint:'Choose "One positive charge", "Released from rest" and a positive test charge, then wait.'},attracted:{title:"Pulled in",description:"Around one positive charge, release a negative test charge and let it fall onto the charge.",hint:"A negative test charge goes against the field: towards the positive charge."},strong:{title:"A very strong field",description:"Hold the test charge in place where the field is 5000 kN/C or more.",hint:"The field grows as 1/r\xB2: move the test charge close to a large charge."},zero:{title:"A place with no field",description:"Between two positive charges, hold the test charge at a point where the field is (almost) zero.",hint:'Choose "Two positive charges". By symmetry the fields cancel exactly in the middle: (0, 0).'},uniform:{title:"The same field everywhere",description:"Between two charged plates, hold the test charge at least 6 cm from the middle, yet where the field is within 3 % of the field in the middle.",hint:'Choose "Two charged plates" and put the test charge inside the gap, to the side but far from the plates.'},dipoleRun:{title:"From + to \u2212",description:"Around a dipole, release a positive test charge and let it run all the way to the negative charge.",hint:"Start on the line between the charges (for example in the middle) \u2014 the field points from + to \u2212."}}}},i={sim:a};export{i as default,a as sim};
